{
  "result": "There is a specific hour on Sunday afternoons that feels borrowed. It's around 4:30, when the light gets flat and yellow, and the whole week has already exhaled but the next one hasn't inhaled yet. As a kid, I hated that hour. It felt like being left on read by the universe.\n\nNow I like it.\n\nI went to the grocery store last Sunday at exactly that time. The place was almost empty. An old song was playing over the speakers, something my mom used to hum while doing dishes, but I couldn't quite name. The produce guy was misting the lettuce, and for a second the water caught the light and made a brief, low rainbow over the kale. Nobody else saw it.\n\nI think much of adult life is learning to notice those small, unassigned moments of beauty that aren't trying to sell you anything or impress you. They just happen. The steam from a manhole cover on a cold morning. How your cat makes biscuits on your sweater like she's trying to pay rent. The way a good book smells colder than the rest of the shelf.\n\nWe spend so much energy chasing the big moments \u2014 graduations, proposals, promotions \u2014 that we forget the texture is actually in the grout between them. The unspectacular, ordinary miracle of a quiet Sunday.\n\nI bought oranges I didn't need, just to stay in that light a little longer. It was worth it. Some light deserves to be lingered in, slow and deliberate.",
